<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Loon</h1><p class="st-meta">1015 CE – 1366 CE (approx.) · capital Borgloon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Limburg county around Borgloon and Hasselt that remained independent for three centuries before being absorbed by the prince-bishops of Liège.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgloon">Loon</a> appear around 1015, when Giselbert held lands around Borgloon in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesbaye">Hesbaye</a>. They expanded north into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campine">Kempen</a> heathlands, and the county became one of the middle-ranking principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Lower Lorraine</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgloon">Loon</a> was largely rural, with small towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasselt">Hasselt</a> and Borgloon. From the late 12th century the counts were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Liège</a>. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henric-van-veldeke">Henric van Veldeke</a>, a founder of literature in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and German, came from the county and wrote for its court circles.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Count Louis IV died childless in 1336, his inheritance was disputed between his heirs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Liège</a>. In 1366 the Prince-Bishopric of Liège annexed the county, and it remained part of the principality until 1795; it corresponds roughly to Belgian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-limburg">Limburg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-loon.jpg" alt="County of Loon" width="320" height="211"><figcaption>Image: Alphathon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1250_Flandern_Brabant_Hennegau_Loon_L%C3%BCttich_Limburg_J%C3%BClich_K%C3%B6ln_Berg_Namur_Chiny_Luxemburg_Trier.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1015 CE – 1366 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Borgloon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Giselbert of Loon, Louis IV of Loon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Looz, Loon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1190&amp;focus=county-of-loon" aria-label="County of Loon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4802 415 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Loon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5122 671L5229 694M5122 671L5127 676"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5127 676h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5122 671h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5122 671h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Borgloon. <a href="/atlas?year=1190&amp;focus=county-of-loon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
